import{_ as t,o,c as a,al as n}from"./chunks/framework.BKGVwAqU.js";const g=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Systems Thinking & Pattern Matching","description":"Systems Thinking & Pattern Matching: Recognizing Archetypes, Developing Instinct. A chapter of VERA's operating philosophy for agency owners, by Tom Hart.","frontmatter":{"title":"Systems Thinking & Pattern Matching","order":9},"headers":[],"relativePath":"about/chapters/09-systems-thinking-pattern-matching.md","filePath":"about/chapters/09-systems-thinking-pattern-matching.md"}`),i={name:"about/chapters/09-systems-thinking-pattern-matching.md"};function s(r,e,h,l,c,p){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h3 id="_09-systems-thinking-pattern-matching" tabindex="-1">09.<br>Systems Thinking &amp; Pattern Matching <a class="header-anchor" href="#_09-systems-thinking-pattern-matching" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;09.&lt;br&gt;Systems Thinking &amp; Pattern Matching&quot;">​</a></h3><blockquote><p>&quot;Vision paints the picture of what we want to create. Systems thinking reveals how we have created what we currently have.&quot;</p><p>The Fifth Discipline, Peter Senge</p></blockquote><h4 id="recognizing-archetypes-developing-instinct" tabindex="-1">Recognizing Archetypes, Developing Instinct <a class="header-anchor" href="#recognizing-archetypes-developing-instinct" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recognizing Archetypes, Developing Instinct&quot;">​</a></h4><p>We tend to overvalue words and overlook behavior. Words tend to have too much power over us, muddling our ability to understand people&#39;s choices. When someone disappoints us we want to chalk it up to their being a victim of their circumstances rather than looking for behavioral patterns: a repeating habit that would challenge us to make braver decisions of our own. We reassure ourselves that we&#39;re measuring the performance of our expectations against our results — generously handing out KPIs, OKRs, SMART goals, etc. — but in the cold light of day we&#39;re just going through the motions.</p><p>Let&#39;s remove the burden of cooking up a recipe of goals we hope will steer complex improvements in our people. We can do better by keeping things simpler.</p><h5 id="measure-behaviors-not-words" tabindex="-1">Measure Behaviors, Not Words <a class="header-anchor" href="#measure-behaviors-not-words" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Measure Behaviors, Not Words&quot;">​</a></h5><p>By making our expectations crystal clear it&#39;s fair to judge behaviors which either satisfy what we want (or fail to do so). Some examples to illustrate what I mean:</p><ul><li><em>I expect you to be proactive, to use your time wisely, and bring only problems to me which you&#39;ve tried to solve but cannot</em></li><li><em>I want to see people helping each other, not covering their asses. If you&#39;re spending time defending yourself you&#39;re wasting time by not helping your team</em></li><li><em>Good ideas can come from anywhere; I expect you all to contribute, not to wait for me to decide</em></li></ul><p>More than any Key Performance Indicator, these measures (of behavior) will tell you exactly who &#39;gets it&#39; and who will struggle to last. Measuring performance is not supposed to be a Hunger Games-style game show designed to sort the weak from the strong, it&#39;s meant to measure professional growth. Overly complicated job descriptions that list every possible responsibility under the sun begin the owner-operator&#39;s confusing struggle. Overly complicated measures of success continue that chore, burdening management with surveiling their own people instead of providing essential, honest feedback employees can immediately use for their own growth.</p><blockquote><p>&quot;The problem was [...] the tendency of managers to confront complex business issues with &#39;9-point programs,&#39; as if the problem is fixing a flat tire.&quot;</p><p>The Fifth Discipline, Peter Senge</p></blockquote><p>It&#39;s dishonest to ignore how complex work has become, but simple approaches to complex problems need not be reductive.</p><h5 id="kill-fake-urgency" tabindex="-1">Kill Fake Urgency <a class="header-anchor" href="#kill-fake-urgency" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Kill Fake Urgency&quot;">​</a></h5><p>Patrick Lencioni was wrong when he said: &quot;If everything is important nothing is important,&quot; and the reason matters. Let&#39;s accept two things about &quot;important&quot; and &quot;urgent&quot; in the modern workplace:</p><ol><li>Every task is important (we wouldn&#39;t do it if it wasn&#39;t)</li><li>Not every task is urgent (some things can wait, others cannot wait)</li></ol><p>We cannot get everything done urgently, so it&#39;s vital to triage what needs to happen immediately and what comes after what&#39;s most urgent. Everything being important, we need to avoid fake urgency that <em>feels</em> like we&#39;re getting something done when we&#39;re really just spinning our wheels without any real traction. &quot;If everything is <s>important</s> <strong>urgent</strong> nothing is <s>important</s> <strong>urgent</strong>.&quot;</p><h5 id="stop-reacting-start-creating" tabindex="-1">Stop Reacting, Start Creating <a class="header-anchor" href="#stop-reacting-start-creating" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stop Reacting, Start Creating&quot;">​</a></h5><blockquote><p>&quot;The dominance of the &#39;event mentality&#39; tells people that the name of the game is reacting to change, not generating change.&quot;</p><p>The Fifth Discipline, Peter Senge</p></blockquote><p>There are too many reasons to list why small companies find themselves in a reactionary mindset on the average workday. In most cases it begins at the point of sale, where a prospect, seeking reassurance, looks you in the eye and asks you if they&#39;re going to regret their purchase. Payroll, never being more than two weeks away, looms large in your mind as you say <em>we will move heaven and earth to make your dreams come true</em>. It is absolutely natural to want to satisfy your new client. Besides, no one on your team knows the full extent of your promises, so what&#39;s the harm? Fast forward six months and:</p><ul><li>Fingers are pointed on both sides about why the project is late</li><li>Only half of the work has been done; all the budget is spent</li><li>Relationships inside and out are strained</li></ul><p>What&#39;s the alternative? Systems thinking. Pattern matching. Introspection. Honesty. <em>That sounds great, what do those things look and feel like?</em></p><ul><li>When people doing the work tell you they feel uneasy about the way things are going, ask them to elaborate</li><li>When the client misses a meeting, label it as a problem with the client</li><li>When the client is unhappy with our work, create space and time to talk through internally whether this was an avoidable, complex, or intelligent mistake; don&#39;t assume any single point of failure</li><li>When someone on our team falls below our standard — cameras on; good lighting; a positive demeanor is mandatory — we address that head on, immediately, with the individual (especially if that&#39;s the CEO)</li><li>Meetings used to be much snappier and useful. They&#39;ve slipped into deep-dives into how frustrating clients are. We need to address the slippage and own the failure</li></ul><p>Operating values are more useful than core values because you can point at them — they&#39;re naked. A core value of <em>&#39;be the change&#39;</em> and an operating value of <em>&#39;give feedback immediately&#39;</em> may have similar intent but one is far simpler to practice.</p><p>Senge&#39;s systems thinking applied to daily professional work. Most situations are 5-6 recurring patterns. Ability to recognize which archetype you&#39;re in, predict consequences, short-circuit for best fit. Kahneman&#39;s System 1 vs System 2: Goal is to develop System 1 pattern matching through experience so you&#39;re not deep-diving constantly. This is mastery. Why AI can follow rules but can&#39;t improvise when patterns break. Human judgment developed through safe failure is irreplaceable. Small firms are messy because of the noise to signal ratio. There&#39;s too much noise to concentrate, and yet our best work happens when we can focus. Insisting on your business being a learning company is the only way to rationalize the noise, to make sense of signals, and to learn from (inevitable) mistakes.</p><hr><blockquote><p>&quot;The art of progress is to preserve order amid change, and to preserve change amid order.&quot;</p><p>— <em>Alfred North Whitehead</em></p></blockquote>',25)])])}const m=t(i,[["render",s]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
